WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.270
Before trusting the real matrix,
use four rows and two features.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.930
These values are an illustrative fixture.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.690
The targets are five, two,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.390
four and eight.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.370
Initial weights are one and one,
with bias zero.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.870
Matrix times weights plus
bias gives predictions one,

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:21.740
one,

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.290
two and one.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:29.570
The first row calculation is one times
one, plus zero times one, plus zero.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.690
Its target is five,
so its residual is negative four.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.390
Residual always means
prediction minus target.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.150
Batch size four differs
from feature count two.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.730
That makes incorrect transpositions easier
to catch than a square fixture would.

00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:51.587
All four residuals are negative four,
negative one,

00:00:51.657 --> 00:00:54.087
negative two and negative seven.

00:00:54.537 --> 00:00:56.507
Squaring and adding gives seventy.

00:00:57.357 --> 00:01:01.647
Mean half squared error divides by twice
the batch count, which is eight here.

00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:04.427
The initial objective is
eight point seven five.

00:01:05.257 --> 00:01:09.287
The factor one half cancels the
two when differentiating a square.

00:01:09.937 --> 00:01:14.097
Averaging prevents the update magnitude
from growing merely because the batch

00:01:14.097 --> 00:01:16.547
contains more copies of
the same observations.

00:01:17.317 --> 00:01:20.747
This objective has squared units
on the real rental problem.

00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:25.107
It is different from the reported mean
absolute error in rentals per hour.

00:01:25.537 --> 00:01:29.197
A training objective and a validation
reporting metric can serve different

00:01:29.197 --> 00:01:29.747
roles.

00:01:31.942 --> 00:01:36.572
Each row contributes its residual
times its feature to a weight gradient.

00:01:37.122 --> 00:01:39.752
Add those contributions,
then divide by four.

00:01:40.602 --> 00:01:42.532
The first coordinate gives negative five.

00:01:42.862 --> 00:01:44.592
The second gives positive one.

00:01:45.122 --> 00:01:49.272
The bias gradient is the average residual,
negative three point five.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.112
Notice the last row.

00:01:51.682 --> 00:01:55.912
Its residual is negative seven and
its second feature is negative one,

00:01:56.242 --> 00:01:59.172
so its contribution to that
weight is positive seven.

00:01:59.662 --> 00:02:03.552
All predictions are too low,
yet one weight gradient is positive.

00:02:04.042 --> 00:02:05.232
Feature signs matter.

00:02:06.062 --> 00:02:07.782
At learning rate zero point one,

00:02:07.782 --> 00:02:12.202
subtracting these gradients gives weights
one point five and zero point nine,

00:02:12.202 --> 00:02:14.112
with bias zero point three five.

00:02:16.483 --> 00:02:21.633
This shortened source trace calculates
residuals, averages both gradients,

00:02:21.763 --> 00:02:23.713
then subtracts the selected step.

00:02:24.323 --> 00:02:25.653
After the first update,

00:02:26.023 --> 00:02:27.933
predictions are one point eight five,

00:02:28.523 --> 00:02:29.493
one point two five,

00:02:30.123 --> 00:02:31.093
two point seven five

00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:32.713
and two point four five.

00:02:33.603 --> 00:02:36.773
The objective falls to five
point three five six two five.

00:02:37.583 --> 00:02:41.933
For the second update, recalculate
residuals from those predictions.

00:02:42.803 --> 00:02:46.563
They are negative three point one five,
negative zero point seven five,

00:02:46.563 --> 00:02:48.953
negative one point two five

00:02:49.483 --> 00:02:51.293
and negative five point five five.

00:02:51.743 --> 00:02:55.273
Reusing the first gradient
would apply a different update.

00:02:55.863 --> 00:02:59.863
The new weight gradients are negative
three point eight seven five and positive

00:02:59.863 --> 00:03:02.173
zero point eight eight seven five.

00:03:04.643 --> 00:03:06.853
Add the four new residuals.

00:03:06.883 --> 00:03:09.713
Their sum is negative ten point seven,

00:03:09.823 --> 00:03:13.733
so the new bias gradient is
negative two point six seven five.

00:03:14.723 --> 00:03:19.903
Subtracting one tenth of that from zero
point three five gives bias zero point six

00:03:19.903 --> 00:03:21.113
one seven five.

00:03:22.283 --> 00:03:24.593
The updated weights are shown beside it.

00:03:25.483 --> 00:03:29.371
These parameters produce the four
displayed predictions and objective three

00:03:29.371 --> 00:03:32.333
point three one eight zero two seven.

00:03:33.443 --> 00:03:37.683
Independent decimal arithmetic caught a
mistaken hand expectation for this bias

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:38.933
before acceptance.

00:03:39.803 --> 00:03:43.463
Central finite differences separately
check the analytic derivatives,

00:03:43.463 --> 00:03:46.873
with a discrepancy below one billionth.

00:03:47.463 --> 00:03:51.203
Known arithmetic and derivative checks
help establish different parts of the

00:03:51.203 --> 00:03:51.873
implementation.

00:03:54.275 --> 00:03:59.665
Now return to the original parameters
and change only the learning rate to one.

00:04:00.155 --> 00:04:05.445
The same gradients give weights six
and zero, with bias three point five.

00:04:05.835 --> 00:04:09.485
Predictions become nine point five,
three point five,

00:04:09.495 --> 00:04:12.385
nine point five and fifteen point five.

00:04:12.855 --> 00:04:16.245
Their mean half squared error
is thirteen point six two five,

00:04:16.775 --> 00:04:19.185
higher than the initial
eight point seven five.

00:04:19.795 --> 00:04:23.365
The derivative was correct,
but the step overshot.

00:04:23.935 --> 00:04:27.365
As another check,
duplicate every fixture row.

00:04:27.795 --> 00:04:33.055
The averaged objective and gradients stay
unchanged because their sums and row count

00:04:33.055 --> 00:04:34.325
double together.

00:04:34.795 --> 00:04:35.965
Correct gradients,

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:40.245
appropriate reduction and a usable
step size are separate requirements.

